WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:07.460
When it comes to in jokes and Easter eggs

00:00:07.470 --> 00:00:11.490
no one does it better than the House of Mouse. Disney movies are full of really

00:00:11.490 --> 00:00:15.719
cool hidden secrets that keep fans
talking for years. Here are just a few

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:17.440
that you may have missed.

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:19.380
JUNGLE BOOK'S BEATLES

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:26.600
In 1967, Disney released its take on the Rudyard Kipling classic The Jungle Book. With its swinging songs, it

00:00:26.609 --> 00:00:30.660
stars jazz musicians Phil Harris and Louis
Prima as Baloo and King Louie

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:35.140
respectively, but it also almost had some
rock'n'roll, if the filmmakers had their

00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:38.860
way that is. As you may have noticed,
the four vultures who sing "That's What

00:00:38.860 --> 00:00:42.969
Friends Are For" have familiar English
accents and mop tops. That's because they

00:00:42.969 --> 00:00:46.680
were modeled after the Fab Four, The Beatles. But according to one of the

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:50.800
movie's composers, The Beatles declined the
invitation to appear in the movie. Maybe

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:52.360
The Rolling Stones would have done it.

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:54.760
GENIE'S END...AT THE BEGINNING

00:00:55.300 --> 00:01:01.260
In 1992's Aladin, audiences meet a short, wacky peddler who introduces the story they're about to see,

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:04.440
and then he's never seen again. Or is he?

00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:09.700
Over the years, fans theorized that the peddler is actually the genie, but after he's been set free

00:01:09.710 --> 00:01:12.440
by the end of the movie. Uh, spoilers.

00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:16.050
The evidence? They both have four fingers
on their hands, they both have the same

00:01:16.050 --> 00:01:20.230
curly beards, and they're both voiced
by Robin Williams. But after years of

00:01:20.230 --> 00:01:24.650
silence on the theory, Aladin's co-directors, Ron Clements and John Musker, confirmed that in

00:01:24.650 --> 00:01:29.970
2015 that, yes, the peddler and the genie
are one in the same. In this case, fans

00:01:29.970 --> 00:01:31.450
wishes finally came true.

00:01:31.940 --> 00:01:33.360
SIMBA'S PARENTS

00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:40.680
Everyone knows that
James Earl Jones provided the booming voice for Mufassa, Simba's father, in 1994's The Lion King.

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:46.000
Jones is also well known for his role as King Jaffe Joffer, father of Eddie Murphy's prince Akeem, in

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:51.300
1988's Coming To America. What most fans
probably don't know however, is that King

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:56.740
Joffer's royal wife, Queen Aoleon, was played
by actress Madge Sinclair, and Sinclair

00:01:56.740 --> 00:02:01.180
also provided the voice for Simba's
mother, Sarabi. Clearly Jones and Sinclair

00:02:01.180 --> 00:02:02.940
were a perfect royal match.

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:06.020
HERCULES' HAUNTING MUSES

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:11.020
Disney movies are so filled with references that some of them can slip by in a flash.

00:02:11.030 --> 00:02:14.660
One such example comes in 1997's Hercules, which features a

00:02:14.660 --> 00:02:18.880
quintet of the singing muses who move
the story along. During Meg's song,

00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:23.340
"I Won't Say I'm In Love," the muses take the form of five stone busts, and they're

00:02:23.350 --> 00:02:27.820
arranged exactly like five similar stone busts, known as the Grim Grinning Ghosts

00:02:27.820 --> 00:02:30.100
from Disney's Haunted Mansion ride.

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:32.460
THE BEST WAY TO TAME A LION

00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:37.680
Lots of Disney characters make brief, unexpected cameos in other characters' films, but usually in

00:02:37.690 --> 00:02:41.750
the form of a blink-and-you'll-miss-it appearance
in the background, but not always. In the

00:02:41.750 --> 00:02:45.610
original Greek myth, Hercules is tasked
with fighting and slaying the Nemean

00:02:45.610 --> 00:02:49.570
Lion, a fearsome beast that no
mortal can kill. While we don't see

00:02:49.570 --> 00:02:53.510
Hercules fighting the lion in the animated
movie, we definitely see that fight's

00:02:53.510 --> 00:02:58.630
loser, the villainous Scar from The Lion King. Too bad Mufasa never thought of that.

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:01.860
BIG HERO 6'S BIG, HEROIC CAMEO

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:05.680
Even though Big Hero 6 was a smash hit in 2014,

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:08.740
a lot of Disney fans might not know that
it was actually the first

00:03:08.740 --> 00:03:13.500
Disney animated film to feature Marvel
Comics characters. Big Hero 6 was

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:18.160
originally a comic book that debuted in
1998, but what's really interesting is

00:03:18.170 --> 00:03:22.890
the cameo featuring the most legendary
Marvel character of them all, Stan Lee.

00:03:22.890 --> 00:03:27.859
In the movie's post credits scene, Fred and
his dad bond over their, uh, mutual love

00:03:27.859 --> 00:03:31.999
of wearing their underpants four days in a
row. You know, as one does.

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:36.239
True Marvel zombies can't miss the fact that
Fred's dad is voiced by and modeled

00:03:36.239 --> 00:03:40.719
after Lee, co-creator of Spider-Man, The
Fantastic Four, The Hulk, Iron Man, and

00:03:40.719 --> 00:03:44.750
countless others. Even though he's been
in nearly every Marvel movie since X-Men

00:03:44.750 --> 00:03:48.450
in 2000, this is probably the one that
feels the most super.

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:50.620
WRECK-IT RALPH'S GRAFFITI ARTISTS

00:03:50.940 --> 00:03:53.940
There's no shortage of
cameos from famous video game characters

00:03:53.959 --> 00:03:58.530
throughout 2012's Wreck-It Ralph. From King
Koopa, to Zangief, to Sonic the

00:03:58.530 --> 00:04:02.700
Hedgehog, gamers were treated to tons of
appearances from heroes and villains who

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:06.150
would otherwise probably never get to
share a screen together. But more than that,

00:04:06.150 --> 00:04:09.810
the world of Wreck-It Ralph has some even
deeper cuts that only the most

00:04:09.810 --> 00:04:14.900
dedicated fans can spot. When Fix-It Felix and
Sergeant Calhoun prepare to enter Sugar

00:04:14.909 --> 00:04:20.260
Rush, graffiti referencing famous games
and in jokes line the walls.There's "Aerith lives,"

00:04:20.260 --> 00:04:25.220
referencing Final Fantasy 7, there's
a nod to the infamous Leroy Jenkins, as

00:04:25.220 --> 00:04:27.810
well as a tag saying "Sheng Long was here,"

00:04:27.810 --> 00:04:32.080
referencing Street Fighter 2's most
important non-character. And, of course,

00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:36.680
no gaming references would be complete
without "all your base are belong to us."

00:04:36.680 --> 00:04:40.480
Seems that the street artists in the
world of games love a good meme as much as we do.

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:43.360
THE ALL-POWERFUL KONAMI CODE

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:48.680
Out of all of Wreck-It Ralph's many references, there's one that truly stands out among them all.

00:04:48.680 --> 00:04:54.880
When the villainous King Candy starts to enter the secret vault to hack Sugar Rush's code, he pulls out a napkin

00:04:54.880 --> 00:04:56.980
with mysterious instructions written on it:

00:04:56.980 --> 00:05:03.580
Up, up, down, down, left, right, left, right, B, A, Start. If you know what that is,

00:05:03.580 --> 00:05:07.780
congratulations, you played Nintendo games
in the '80s. That sequence of

00:05:07.780 --> 00:05:11.919
commands is known as the Konami Code, and
it first appeared as a way to get power

00:05:11.919 --> 00:05:17.509
ups and extra lives in the NES games Gradius and Contra back in 1986, both made

00:05:17.509 --> 00:05:21.930
by Japanese developer Konami. The code has
since taken on mythical status among

00:05:21.930 --> 00:05:26.210
fans, and it's found its way into plenty
of other games, and even websites.

00:05:26.500 --> 00:05:28.000
BACK TO SCHOOL

00:05:28.140 --> 00:05:32.280
If you keep your eyes sharp, you'll
notice a recurring combination of letters and numbers,

00:05:32.280 --> 00:05:40.400
A113, that keeps appearing in lots of Disney's movies. It shows up in Meet the Robinsons, The
Princess and the Frog, The Brave Little

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:43.300
Toaster, and Lilo and Stitch, just to name
a few.

00:05:43.300 --> 00:05:48.349
It also happens to appear in every
single Pixar movie. Why? Well, that's just

00:05:48.349 --> 00:05:51.889
the classroom number for the first year
graphic design and character animation

00:05:51.889 --> 00:05:56.199
class at the California Institute of Arts,
where many of the animators who've worked at

00:05:56.200 --> 00:06:00.440
Disney and Pixar started out. The code
even makes appearances in non-Disney

00:06:00.440 --> 00:06:04.940
properties, like episodes of Family Guy
and The Simpsons. So aspiring animators

00:06:04.940 --> 00:06:09.400
take note: if you want to get a job in
showbiz, now you know exactly where to

00:06:09.400 --> 00:06:10.800
send your application.

00:06:24.680 --> 00:06:27.910
Thanks for watching! Don't forget to like
and subscribe if you want more.

